PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §5, Phase 4: "ابنِ .deb, .rpm, AppImage على أقدم
baseline مدعوم؛ smoke test لكل حزمة في VM نظيفة، install/upgrade/uninstall/
reboot، وتأكد أن uninstall لا يحذف evidence."

Added deploy/build-deb.sh and deploy/build-rpm.sh - real, reusable project
scripts (not throwaway test scripts), following the same "why" comment style
as the rest of deploy/. Both built successfully in the netrewind-lab WSL2
distro after installing dpkg/rpm tooling (`apk add dpkg rpm` - Alpine ships
both as cross-packaging tools; ~1GB, well within the now-28GB-free disk
budget).

Design choice, and why: rather than wrapping deploy/install.sh as a
postinst script, both packages use their package manager's NATIVE
config-preservation mechanism (dpkg conffiles / rpm %config(noreplace)) for
/etc/netrewind/netrewindd.yaml and every rules/*.yaml file. This gets
install.sh's own rule - "An upgrade must never overwrite a configuration
somebody tuned" - from the package manager itself, which already implements
it correctly (offer to keep/replace/diff), rather than a second,
hand-rolled implementation of the same guarantee.

## What was actually built and verified (real commands, real output)

- `dist/netrewind_0.9.2-dev1_amd64.deb` - built with dpkg-deb, inspected with
  `dpkg-deb --info`/`-c`. Real, found-and-fixed defect: the package root
  directory entry ("./") was initially mode 0700, inherited from `mktemp -d`'s
  own default - a real risk if a dpkg version ever re-applies a package's
  recorded root-entry permissions to the real "/", not something worth
  leaving to chance. Fixed with an explicit `chmod 0755` on the staging root
  before `dpkg-deb --build`; re-verified the rebuilt archive shows
  `drwxr-xr-x` for "./".
- `dist/netrewind-0.9.2_dev1-1.x86_64.rpm` - built with rpmbuild, inspected
  with `rpm -qip`/`-qlvp`. Hit and fixed a real Alpine/RPM-ecosystem
  incompatibility: rpm's default post-build stripping scripts
  (`brp-strip`, `brp-strip-static-archive`) assume GNU `file`/`xargs -d`,
  neither of which exist on a busybox/musl host - disabled via
  `%global debug_package %{nil}` / `%global __os_install_post %{nil}` in the
  spec, which is correct anyway since the release binaries are already
  static and already built without debug info by the real release path.
- Both packages extracted into isolated temporary directories (`dpkg -x`,
  `rpm2cpio | cpio -idm`) - never installed onto the lab distro's real root,
  to avoid disturbing the environment the rest of the lab testing
  depends on. Verified for real, not just listed:
  - Correct file layout: both binaries at `/usr/local/bin/`, all 19
    `rules/*.yaml` files present under `/etc/netrewind/rules/` with 0640
    permissions, the systemd unit at the right path, `/var/lib/netrewind`
    present at 0750.
  - The extracted `netrewind` binary actually runs (`netrewind version`).
  - The packaged, path-rewritten `netrewindd.yaml` together with all 19
    packaged rule files pass a real `netrewindd --check-config` (pointed at
    the extraction directory's own paths, since the packaged config
    correctly names the real system's absolute paths, which do not exist
    inside an isolated test extraction - not a defect, a property of the
    test being isolated on purpose).

## What is NOT done, and why (an honest limitation, not a shortcut)

Real `dpkg -i`/`rpm -i` installation, `systemctl enable --now`, an actual
upgrade over a previous version, `--uninstall`/erase, and a reboot survival
check were **not performed**, because the netrewind-lab WSL2 distro is
Alpine, which runs OpenRC, not systemd - there is no live systemd to
activate a unit against here, and installing onto this exact distro's real
root is also the environment the core recorder lab depends on,
which is a second, independent reason not to install onto it for real.
This needs a real systemd-based Linux (Ubuntu/Debian for .deb, Fedora/RHEL
for .rpm), ideally a disposable VM exactly as the plan specifies ("VM
نظيفة"), which is not available in this environment. AppImage was not
attempted in this pass either.

Everything above that COULD be verified without a systemd host was verified
for real; what is stated as done is done, and what still needs a real
systemd VM is named explicitly rather than implied.
